    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mehrgarh is one of the earliest known places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> where people farmed barley and wheat and herded sheep, goats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> houses.</li><li>Over thousands of years its villagers moved from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> life without pottery to wheel-made decorated wares, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> working, faience <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and seals.</li><li>Whether its farming came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> or grew locally is debated, and so is the age of its first village: c. 7000 BCE in the older dating, c. 5250 BCE in the newer.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Eleven molar crowns from nine adults buried at Mehrgarh had been drilled during life: the oldest evidence of its kind, taken as a sign of a long tradition of proto-dentistry among early farmers.</p></aside>
    </section>

